    Sample 01 - a long-form article: the reading-mode case. Headings, running prose, a pull
    quote, lists and links; no code, no tables. Everything here is intentionally CLASSLESS -
    the whole point of the designer is that the wrapper styles raw HTML.
*@
<h1>The type you never wrote</h1>

<p>Most of the text an application renders was never marked up by hand. It arrives from a
markdown file, a CMS field, a translation bundle, or a model streaming tokens into a chat
window. It has no classes, no design review, and no one watching it - and it is usually the
text your users actually read.</p>

<p>A component library covers the buttons and the dialogs. The paragraph between them is on
its own. That gap is what a typeset closes: one stylesheet that takes ownership of every
element you did not write, and styles it with the same tokens the rest of the interface
already uses.</p>

<h2>Three knobs, not thirty</h2>

<p>Typography systems fail by offering too much. A size for every heading, a margin for every
pairing, a line-height per breakpoint - each one a decision, each decision a place to drift.
A typeset condenses the whole surface to three values:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Size</strong> (<code>--typeset-size</code>) sets the body text; every heading
        and caption derives from it.</li>
    <li><strong>Leading</strong> (<code>--typeset-leading</code>) sets the line-height of
        running prose.</li>
    <li><strong>Flow</strong> (<code>--typeset-flow</code>) sets the space between blocks;
        sections, lists and rules scale their own gaps from it.</li>
</ul>

<p>Everything else is arithmetic. Change the flow and the headings breathe with it. Drop the
size into a chat bubble and the whole hierarchy shrinks in step, because it was never absolute
to begin with.</p>

<blockquote>
    <p>Good typography is invisible. You only notice it when someone turns it off.</p>
</blockquote>

<h2>Owned, not installed</h2>

<p>The stylesheet is a file in your repository, not a package in your lock file. When the
sizes feel wrong for your product, you edit the file. When a new element needs styling, you
add a rule. There is nothing to configure because there is nothing between you and the CSS.</p>

<p>And because every rule carries zero specificity, escaping is ordinary: put a utility class
on any element and it wins. Need a subtree left alone entirely? Mark it
<code>not-typeset</code>. The prose layer never argues with the component layer - it fills the
space beneath it.</p>

<h2>Where it earns its keep</h2>

<p>Documentation is the obvious home, but the sharper case is streaming. A chat transcript
appends blocks while the user watches; a spacing system built on last-child margins restyles
the whole message every time a paragraph lands. A typeset spaces blocks only from above, so
what is on screen never shifts - the next block simply arrives.</p>

<p>The same file covers both. Wrap the article, wrap the bubble, and tune each with a preset
class: reading mode gets room, conversation gets density, and the type stays one voice.</p>
